title: Pruning the log
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: guide
aliases: prune
summary: mf log prune before deletes every operation older than a target. It is irreversible.

```
mf -n music log list                           # look first: what would go
mf -n music log prune before before-cleanup    # everything older than that label goes
mf -n music log prune before --id 55
mf -n music log prune before --timestamp 2026-09-01T00:00:00Z
```

''Irreversible'': deleted operations cannot be reverted any more. It asks for confirmation
(unless `--force`; `--silent` does not skip it). The target — a label, `--id` or
`--timestamp` — is kept; every operation strictly older goes, with the revisions left
empty. `Pruned 47 operations across 3 revisions.`

Nothing else depends on what is pruned: a revert applies an operation's inverse onto the
current state, so every operation left can still be reverted ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). Undo will not go past the oldest operation left. For disaster recovery, rely on a
backup of the store ([[Backups and restore]]), not on the log.

The space pruning frees is reused by the store's next writes; a backup is a compacted
copy. The retention limit does the same automatically ([[Automatic retention]]).

`POST /repos/:repo/log/prune` with `{"target": {"id": N} | {"timestamp": T} | {"label":
"…"}}` answers `{"pruned_operations", "pruned_revisions"}`.
